  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nAn area of low pressure is beginning to form near Seattle and \nVancouver. This system will work its way southward through Thursday, \nbringing in cooler than average temperatures, breezy conditions \nacross the mountains and deserts, and a healthier marine layer. Low \nclouds will fill in much of the coastal basin each night and morning \nwith patchy fog on higher terrain. Models hint that the atmosphere \nmay be moist enough to produce some areas of drizzle Wednesday \nmorning within 10-15 miles of the coast.\n\nModels pinpoint the low moving as far as Northern California by \nThursday. This will bring temperatures down near 5 to 10 degrees \nbelow normal for inland areas. Chances continue to increase to see \nhighs near 85 or cooler for areas of the High Desert, Inland Empire \nand Inland San Diego Co valleys on Wednesday and Thursday. Sea \nbreeze winds will increase over desert passes and deserts as well \neach afternoon, primarily between 20 and 40 MPH. \n\nThe low lifts out of the State and into the Northern Rockies by \nFriday into the weekend. This will lead to subtle warming by Friday \ninto Saturday as a quick and weak area of high pressure nudges into \nthe region. High temperatures will respond by increasing slightly \nand emerging closer to normal by Saturday. Models are beginning to \ncome into better agreement of a weaker area of low pressure moving \nover the area by early next week. This would begin to cool the \nregion by a few degrees, giving us near to slightly below average \ntemperatures once again.",
